  "onboardingPasteGrantCta": "Allow auto-paste",
  "onboardingPasteSkip": "Just copy for now, no auto-insert",
  "onboardingPasteWhyMac": "Without permission, your text is copied to the clipboard. You'll then paste manually with ⌘V.",
  "onboardingPasteWhyWin": "Auto-Paste presses Ctrl+V for you after each recording, so the text lands right where you're typing. Verified working on this machine — turn it on below.",
  "onboardingPasteWhyWinUipi": "In programs running as administrator, Auto-Paste won't work. The text then stays on the clipboard, and you paste it yourself with Ctrl+V.",
  "onboardingPasteWinOnTitle": "Auto-Paste is on",
  "onboardingPasteWinOnDetail": "After each recording, WhisPaste presses Ctrl+V for you — the text lands right at your cursor. A copy stays on the clipboard, too.",
  "onboardingPasteLinuxNative": "Auto-paste needs your permission for the virtual keyboard (/dev/uinput). With the .deb package, choose “Allow (administrator password)” and enter your administrator password; otherwise copy the command and run it once in a terminal. You can remove the permission again in the settings.",
  "onboardingPasteLinuxFlatpak": "In the Flatpak build, --device=all makes /dev/input/* and /dev/uinput visible inside the sandbox. WhisPaste itself reads no input devices. If your user has read rights on the host (e.g. as a member of the input group), the app could read keystrokes of other programs. Auto-paste also needs write access to /dev/uinput on the host: copy the command and run it once in a terminal. It installs the udev rule 71-whispaste-uinput.rules, which gives access only to the signed-in session. The command needs administrator rights (sudo) and changes the system. If you do not want this, leave auto-paste off and paste the text by hand.",
  "onboardingPasteLinuxSnap": "In the Snap build, run this once in a terminal: sudo snap connect whispaste:uinput. Until then, auto-paste only copies the text.",
  "onboardingPasteLinuxAppImage": "The AppImage needs access to /dev/uinput (the virtual keyboard device). Copy the command and run it once in a terminal. It installs the udev rule 71-whispaste-uinput.rules into /etc/udev/rules.d/ and runs sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=misc --sysname-match=uinput. The rule gives write access only to the signed-in session. The command needs administrator rights (sudo) and changes the system. Until then, auto-paste only copies the text.",
  "onboardingPasteWinEnableCta": "Turn on Auto-Paste",
  "onboardingPasteWinAdminCaveat": "In programs running as administrator, Auto-Paste won't work. The text then stays on the clipboard, and you paste it yourself with Ctrl+V.",
  "onboardingPasteLinuxClipboardFallback": "Auto-Paste is on. Until access is set up, WhisPaste puts the text on the clipboard, and you paste it yourself with Ctrl+V.",
  "onboardingPasteLinuxUnavailableDetail": "The text goes to the clipboard. Paste it with {shortcut}.",
